Subject: [PATCH] Batch 8a: global legal citation index + D64 citation-identity
 conflict (CT33)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The engine's only legal-reference check was D22's 'section number above 500' —
it never read the citations themselves. The app runs in every jurisdiction, so
this batch builds the citation layer jurisdiction-neutral:

- voExtractCitationIndex: every statute and case the record cites, verbatim,
  with page and character span — Commonwealth 'Act N of YYYY', UK 'Act YYYY',
  U.S.C./C.F.R., US national + regional reporters, EU instruments and CJEU
  numbers, Indian AIR/SCC, SA law reports and CCT numbers, and the bracketed
  neutral citation used across UK/SA/AU/CA/NZ/SG/HK/IE. Unknown court codes
  are indexed and marked 'unknown', never dropped. Sealed inside the findings
  JSON (v1.12.0) like the Batch 7a conduct ledger.
- D64 (CT33): fires only where one instrument name carries two different
  statutory identifiers (number+year = a unique pair). Deliberate-version
  guard ('the repealed Companies Act 61 of 1973') silences records discussing
  two versions; case citations never conflict (parallel citation is normal);
  bare-year UK-style acts are index-only (two real Acts share one name).
- D22 narrowed by the sealed index: the ContractNLI precision probe (61 clean
  Stanford NDAs) caught it firing on 'Section 1159 of the Companies Act 2006'
  — a real provision. Bare-year long-form Acts no longer hit the >500 floor;
  the Commonwealth numbered form keeps it; no index = original behaviour.

Boundaries: the index never asserts a citation exists in the real world;
bare 'the Act' references stay with the advisory layer.

benchmarks/contractnli.mjs (dev tool): precision probe + citation yield over
the ContractNLI dev split (dataset not committed). Baseline: CT33 1 -> 0 on
clean NDAs; remaining CT23/CT57/CT53 hits logged as FP-review candidates.

Engine v5.8.0-web; 62 detectors; 55 suites, 3531 assertions green; npm run
check clean; inline copies re-spliced byte-exact.
